    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1861eb83_85f32095",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/ion.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 61,
      "author": {
        "id": 1530208
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-28T18:27:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this might be confusing to our clients, because when they construct their bitmasks for allocation, they do something like\n\nheap_id_mask \u003d 0;\nheap_id_mask \u003d ION_HEAP(SYSTEM_HEAP_ID) | ION_HEAP(CMA_HEAP_1) | ION_HEAP(CMA_HEAP_2), where\n\n#define ION_HEAP(heap_id) (1 \u003c\u003c heap_id).\n\nTherefore, can we define these as simply IDs?",
      "revId": "93a8029497b5344a47d0a9b868ecd6984ec21804",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c9f5ce53_55981741",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/ion.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 61,
      "author": {
        "id": 1108565
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-28T20:49:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I guess, having them as individual numbers make the assign_heap_id code easy too (no more ffs(ion_heap_xxx)).\nBut I don\u0027t like that bit-shifting is an operation that clients have to do. That seems weird in terms of having an API for clients.\n\nThe ION_HEAP() macro does no type checks etc, so its asking for trouble too and we would need more error checks.\n\nWouldn\u0027t it just be nice for clients to do - \n\nion_alloc_fd(..., ION_HEAP_CMA1 | ION_HEAP_CMA2..) instead, so I am keeping this now, but will fix the stupid \"-1\"",
      "parentUuid": "1861eb83_85f32095",
      "revId": "93a8029497b5344a47d0a9b868ecd6984ec21804",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3a74997b_72a21bef",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/ion.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 66,
      "author": {
        "id": 1412036
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-28T18:45:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So let\u0027s say if vendor A uses dma_start to dma_end as vendor A specific CMA heaps and vendor B uses dma_start to dma_end as vendor B specific CMA heaps - in this case how will system image be able to use any of the (dma_end - dma_start + 1) * 2 heaps as they aren\u0027t mandated to be available across vendors?\n\nThis does restrict and/or standardize the number of CMA heaps that can be used by vendors.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 66,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 66,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "93a8029497b5344a47d0a9b868ecd6984ec21804",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "54e226c0_f5736613",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/ion.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 66,
      "author": {
        "id": 1108565
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-28T20:49:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see ..\n\nWell in that case the CMA heap shouldn\u0027t even be a standard heap and then Android can\u0027t rely on it (vendor processes can). I thought you need the CMA heap for android, so we have this.\nThere\u0027s also another problem, how does Android know which heap id to use (even if they were all available). So, I guess if we want to keep this as is, we can specify which exact heap ids are used\nby Android and those are the only ones that are *expected* to exist. I hate that we have to do that manually, but its doable.\n\nThe only other solution I can think of is to make DMA heaps \"custom\" and in that case, its tricky to show exported symbols from CMA etc. Like I said, lets put enough bandaid to make us last for a couple of years, and solve it properly with the *new and shiny* thing.\n\n(We can manage the android code not relying on CMA heaps as long as that\u0027s a general system image rule)",
      "parentUuid": "3a74997b_72a21bef",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 66,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 66,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "93a8029497b5344a47d0a9b868ecd6984ec21804",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    }
